WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.900
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.080
Australia is home to some truly nightmarish
creations.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.919
And no, I don’t mean Neighbours, which has
inexplicably been on British TV for over thirty

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:12.919
years.

00:00:12.919 --> 00:00:14.469
I’m talking about wildlife.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:20.320
The kind you can only find in the land down
under; snakes that can kill you in an instant,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.740
spiders the size of your head, and a variety
of other creatures that would rip out your

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.610
intestines and eat your face as soon as you
look at it.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:34.620
With that in mind, you’d think that if the
Australian Government ever decided to declare

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.489
all-out war on one of the creatures that roam
their beautiful land, it would be on something

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:46.769
venomous, carnivorous, or at the very least
mildly bad tempered.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:48.379
But no.

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:54.499
In perhaps the greatest version of “man
versus beast” ever recorded, the Aussies

00:00:54.499 --> 00:00:58.350
went after the Emu.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:06.840
The ensuing struggle was so bloody it is today
known as the Great Emu war of 1932.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.619
And no, I’m not making that up.

00:01:09.619 --> 00:01:14.430
But you can see why the government would want
to go to town on these fearsome creatures,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.430
can’t you?

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.620
Just look at them!

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:23.570
The baleful, dead eyes, the small-but-probably-very-deadly
beak, the stubby legs that could quite happily

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.049
kick your elderly grandmother to death ... but
actually, the reasons for wanting to commit

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:33.940
mass “emucide” were a bit more complicated
than that.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.149
After World War One, the government decided
to give their discharged veterans some land

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:43.240
in Western Australia so they could live out
their days as farmers, have a bit of peace

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.350
and quiet, and try to forget all about the
horrors of war.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:53.359
It sounded good on paper, but in reality,
it didn’t quite turn out to be the utopia

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:55.500
the veterans had expected.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.079
The land was unfertile and the farming hard,
backbreaking work.

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:05.929
And then the Great Depression arrived in 1929
to make things even worse.

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:11.000
Wheat prices fell precipitously, the government
failed to follow through on promised subsidies

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:19.480
for farmers, and then, in 1932, just to add
insult to injury, the emus turned up.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.349
Twenty thousand of them.

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:28.459
And what was worse, they were hungry for the
sweet taste of man-flesh… well OK wheat.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.810
But still - they were hungry, you could say
they were peckish.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:38.900
Mind you, this wasn’t the first time the
emus had made themselves known.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:39.959
Far from it.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:46.480
Up until 1922, emus had been a protected species,
but they were soon reclassified as “vermin”

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.739
after it became clear they had no respect
for social etiquette.

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:56.660
Emus, you see, don’t have a firm grasp on
the concept of farming.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:02.370
Through the eyes of an emu, a farm is basically
a very convenient kind of all you can eat

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.370
buffet.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:09.769
But farming in Western Australia is hard enough
as it is, so when a horde of weird-looking

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:15.580
birds come along to joyfully munch through
the literal fruits of your labour, you aren’t

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.580
going to take it lying down.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:26.180
In 1923, a bounty system was introduced offering
cash for every emu killed.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.939
It seemed like a good idea at the time, and
it probably would have worked pretty well

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:32.989
if it hadn’t been for the numbers.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:38.840
These ex-military farmers were all well trained
with a rifle, of course, but even with all

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:45.180
their human-killing expertise, they were unable
to slow the wave of feathered enemies heading

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.580
towards them across “no man’s land”
- they simply didn’t have enough ammo.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:57.140
By the time 1932 rolled around, it had become
abundantly clear that unless somebody invented

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:04.590
a bomb that specifically vaporised emus, the
farmers were going to need more guns.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.830
Specifically of the large, fully automatic
variety.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:14.049
Soon enough, several of these ex-soldiers
were knocking on Minister of Defence Sir George

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:21.810
Pearce’s door asking for more firepower:
feather penetrating rounds, beak-seeking missiles,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.479
and of course - a lot more emunition…...

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:28.650
OK, so they didn’t ask for any of those
things.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.990
For reasons I can’t explain, they appear
to have had almost no interest whatsoever

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:33.990
in puns.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:39.050
But they did ask for machine guns with which
to battle their emu opponents.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:44.170
And amazingly, the government agreed, not
only providing the weapons in the form of

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:49.050
two lewis guns, but also sending in the army
to wield them.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.810
The transport of fresh soldiers was paid for
by the Western Australian Government, and

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.860
the farmers provided food, accommodation,
and paid for the ammunition.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.210
It was probably overkill, but as far as Pearce
was concerned, the birds would provide good

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:10.060
target practice for his men, and their feathers
could be used to make hats for the Light Horsemen.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.310
After all, if you’re killing thousands of
birds with two machine guns, you might as

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.750
well kill two birds with one stone while you’re
at it.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:24.580
The operation was led by Major G.P.W Meredith
of the Seventh Heavy Battery of the Royal

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:30.009
Australian Artillery, and if the amount of
syllables in a person’s title are an indication

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:35.820
of their emu-murdering capabilities then he
was certainly the man for the job.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:40.919
In October 1932, he took two soldiers from
his battery – along with two Lewis guns

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:46.200
and ten thousand rounds of ammunition – and
headed off to assist the farmers.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:52.910
During the first day, however, the “war”
was called off by rain, which admittedly makes

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:58.169
it sound more like a tennis match at Wimbledon
than a serious military campaign.

00:05:58.169 --> 00:06:03.820
You couldn't imagine Hitler saying 'hold on
lads, our Panzers are getting damp, let's

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.890
invade Poland tomorrow'.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:12.479
Anyway, this unexpected downpour made the
enemy scatter, which was a significant problem,

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:16.880
because when you’re trying to fire a barrage
of machine gun bullets into a crowd of emus,

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.160
you really don’t want them to start valuing
their personal space.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.639
But by the second of November, hostilities
had resumed once more.

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:29.139
It was at this point Meredith and his men
attempted to herd about fifty emus into a

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:32.770
small area to make sure they were in range
of the guns.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:37.789
But the emus appeared to cotton onto this
plan, and responded by employing their own

00:06:37.789 --> 00:06:43.800
version of“guerrilla tactics”, scattering
into smaller units so they were difficult

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.729
to target en mass.

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:52.100
Even so, a stubby little beak versus a machine
gun isn’t exactly a fair fight, and sadly,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.509
quite a few valiant birds lost their lives
that day.

00:06:55.509 --> 00:07:01.349
But it took so many bullets to do the job
that questions began to be asked as to whether

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:06.440
the whole ‘machine gun the emus’ thing
was economically viable.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.720
Maybe “financial warfare” was part of
the emus’ battle plan all along… although

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.490
they’ve never been known for their accountancy
skills.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:18.590
Either way, Meredith had obviously vastly
underestimated the enemy.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:23.990
Not only were they becoming increasingly difficult
to hit, but when his men did manage to shoot

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.949
one, most of the time the birds seemed to
just take it in their stride.

00:07:26.949 --> 00:07:33.190
So much so that it was taking an average of
ten bullets to bring down a single emu, although

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:37.130
admittedly most of those shots missed their
mark in the first place.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.470
Major Meredith later said: “If we had a
military with the bullet-carrying capacity

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.919
of these birds, it would face any army in
the world.

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:51.681
They can face machine guns with the invulnerability
of tanks” … and if that’s true, why

00:07:51.681 --> 00:07:54.590
were we still using tanks in World War Two?

00:07:54.590 --> 00:08:01.000
Shouldn’t the Allies have ditched the armoured
vehicles and ridden into Berlin on emus instead?

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.419
The war of man versus feathery beast continued,
but frankly, by this point it was a comedy

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:08.789
of errors.

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:14.000
The next thing Meredith did was to set up
an ambush near a local dam, and on the fourth

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.940
of November, over a thousand emus were spotted
heading towards it.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.250
Everything was going swimmingly.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:25.770
All they had to do was wait until those “beaky
bastards” were close enough and open fire.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:32.190
But as the birds finally got within range,
the lewis guns jammed, and only twelve emus

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:35.160
were killed, while the rest legged it.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:42.700
Yet another day in the Great Emu War of 1932
was over without much to write home about.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.080
Meredith was clearly struggling to match wits
with his emu equivalent.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:53.500
And as crazy as it may sound, it really did
seem like the enemy were operating under orders.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.670
In fact, several days into the war, observers
noted that each pack of emus appeared to have

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:04.510
its own general; a big guy, about six foot
high, with black feathers, who called the

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:08.720
shots, and kept watch while the other emus
helped themselves to crops.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:15.340
And is it just me, or do you imagine him wearing
a battle-scarred helmet on his tiny head,

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:20.140
cigar clamped tightly in his beak, and saying
things like: “I love the smell of napalm

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.110
in the morning”?

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:23.110
No?

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.400
Just me then.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:30.000
Out of frustration, or maybe pure desperation,
the army mounted one of the Lewis guns on

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.400
a truck and tried to mow the emus down that
way.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.320
The emus, however, were like Usain Bolt on
speed.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.400
Not only that, but the ride was so bumpy the
gunner in the back was unable to fire off

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.400
any shots.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:48.470
At one point, a particularly brave “kamikaze”
emu managed to get his corpse caught in the

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:53.220
truck’s steering equipment, causing the
driver to steer into a somebody’s fence,

00:09:53.220 --> 00:10:00.080
which promptly disintegrated … and in Western
Australia, fences were pretty damn important

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:04.760
… which was another reason Emus had to be
sacrificed on the altar of agriculture.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:11.480
You see, Emus not only destroyed the crops,
they also left gaps in fences where rabbits

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:12.480
could get through.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.480
“So what?”

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:14.570
I hear you ask.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:18.610
“What’s the big deal about a few cute,
fluffy bunnies?”

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:25.580
Well, rabbits are a very big deal in Oz, hence
the “Rabbit Proof Fence” which runs for

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:29.790
1,700 kilometres across Western Australia.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:34.710
They’re a significant factor in species
loss, and have a huge impact on both natural

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:40.540
resources and crops due to the fact they graze
like a fat man at a wedding.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.770
Mind you, the Rabbit Fence hasn’t done a
fantastic job of bunny-massacring, because

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:46.060
they’re still a problem.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:51.950
In the 1950s, the myxoma virus – which causes
myxomatosis -- was deliberately introduced

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.990
into the rabbit population in Australia.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:59.860
This cut down numbers for a while, but the
rabbits have since developed immunity, allowing

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:04.900
them to keep on breeding like ... well, like
rabbits.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:07.420
Could the Great Rabbit War be next?

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.290
Only time will tell.

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:16.421
By the eighth of November, 1932, two-thousand-five-hundred
shots had been fired in the Great Emu War

00:11:16.421 --> 00:11:19.670
- all of them by the human side, I should
add.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:23.710
But the number of emus killed was only in
the hundreds, with some claiming it was as

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:24.920
low as fifty.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:30.230
Still, it wasn’t all doom and gloom, because
Meredith made a note in his official report

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:35.280
-- sounding particularly proud of himself
-- that not one of his men had been injured

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:36.550
during the battle.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:39.060
Which is a miracle when you think about it.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:44.710
I mean, these poor men were left to face a
mass of placid, flightless birds all on their

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:48.450
own with nothing but machine guns to defend
themselves.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:52.910
It’s a miracle any of those boys got out
alive.

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:57.710
Perhaps unsurprisingly, it was also around
this point Meredith started losing the support

00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.270
of the local media.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:04.330
They were making quite a big deal of the fact
that “only a few emus were dying”, especially

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.990
given the Rambo-esque levels of firepower
being brought to bear.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:12.450
It also didn’t help that serious questions
were being asked in parliament.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:17.290
For example, when one politician suggested
medals should be awarded to those brave soldiers

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:22.330
taking part in the emu war, a member of the
opposition said the medals should be given

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:28.290
to the emus, as they’d “won every round
so far”.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:33.390
If the agricultural ramifications hadn’t
been so serious, Meredith and his men could

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.030
easily have become a laughing stock.

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:39.960
Defence Minister George Pearce, who had agreed
to the whole thing in the first place, was

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:45.480
even given the unofficial title “Minister
for the Emu War,” which could hardly have

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.651
been a feather in the cap of his military
career … Still, he kept on defending the

00:12:50.651 --> 00:12:55.920
operation, and with support from the Premier
of Western Australia it was agreed battle

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.660
would recommence.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:02.540
On the thirteenth of November bullets were
flying again, and by the second of December

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.730
the military were killing around a hundred
emus per week.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:10.240
That was definitely an improvement, but it
was hardly putting a dent in the mass ranks

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:17.960
of emus, and by 1934, the higher-ups in government
were clearly at the end of their feather - I

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:18.960
mean tether.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:20.400
I’ll stop now I promise.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:25.160
The bounty system was reintroduced, and the
farmers were given enough ammunition to take

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.730
care of the issue themselves.

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:31.810
Which proved to be pretty effective, because
over fifty-seven thousand bounties were claimed

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.080
in the following six-months.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:40.310
Mind you, there must still have been a fair
few emus knocking around, because in 1943

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:45.260
wheat farmers were concerned enough to request
military assistance with the growing emu threat

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:46.310
once again.

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:51.970
And, apparently having learned from the debacle
with the lewis guns, this time the farmers

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:56.720
had a better idea - they wanted the army to
bring bombs.

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:58.340
Lot’s of bombs.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:03.850
I’m happy to say the military respectfully
declined this invitation.

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:10.050
But the emu problem wasn’t going away, and
in 1950 it was raised in federal parliament,

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:15.910
where Army Minister Josiah Francis approved
the release of five-hundred-thousand rounds

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.820
of ammunition to help with the claiming of
bounties.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:25.270
All told around three-hundred-thousand emus
were killed in Western Australia as part of

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:29.650
the Great emu war and the skirmishes that
followed.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:33.740
But who really won The Great Emu War of 1932?

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:34.740
Man?

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.110
Or Emu?

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:43.930
It’s true, a lot of emus were sent to meet
their beaky maker, and, so far as we know,

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:46.920
not one human combatant died in the line of
duty.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:53.200
But even so, the emus put up a remarkably
good fight, considering the circumstances.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:57.980
These days, the majestic emu appears on the
Australian coat of arms … which is more

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:01.720
than you can say for Major G.P.W Meredith.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:07.040
And in 2020, a video game was released based
around the Emu War … but I’ve yet to see

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.790
a game set in the Western Australian parliament.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:16.570
Perhaps the greatest victory of all, however,
is that the Emu is still with us, and it’s

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:21.810
thankfully now illegal for anyone to murder
them with machine guns, bombs, or anything

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:23.870
else for that matter.

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:27.810
So, maybe that sarcastic politician was right.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:31.270
Maybe the emus should have been given a medal.

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:37.600
And maybe we should have a day of remembrance
for those emus who lost their lives defending

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.490
their right to eat their own body weight in
wheat.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:45.740
Maybe we can convince Christopher Nolan to
make a movie about it like he did with “Dunkirk”.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:51.990
I’m sure Tom Hardy would make a fantastic
emu.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:16:01.630
Thanks for watching.

